{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "def has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Check if any two numbers in `numbers` are closer to each other than `threshold`.\n\n    Returns True iff there exist i != j such that abs(numbers[i] - numbers[j]) < threshold.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: if threshold is negative, non-finite, or if any element in numbers is NaN.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n    False\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.8, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 2.0], 0.3)\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    # Validate threshold\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be a numeric value\")\n    if not math.isfinite(threshold):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be finite\")\n    if threshold < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be non-negative\")\n\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Validate numbers: reject NaN explicitly (comparisons with NaN are unreliable)\n    for x in numbers:\n        if isinstance(x, float) and math.isnan(x):\n            raise ValueError(\"numbers must not contain NaN\")\n\n    # Sort and check adjacent differences (O(n log n) time, O(n) extra for sorted list)\n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        a = sorted_nums[i - 1]\n        b = sorted_nums[i]\n        # If exactly equal, distance is 0\n        if a == b:\n            if 0.0 < threshold:\n                return True\n            else:\n                continue\n        # Compute difference and check if finite and below threshold\n        diff = b - a\n        if math.isfinite(diff) and diff < threshold:\n            return True\n    return False"}
